paste uses two file-scope FILE objects purely as sentinel addresses in its
array of open streams -- they are never read, written, or handed to stdio.
That needs FILE to be a complete type, which it is in glibc and in the Mes C
library and is not in ntlibc: like musl, ntlibc keeps the stdio object
opaque, so `static FILE dummy_closed;' has no size and does not compile.

Two chars serve the same purpose: the macros only ever produce a pointer
compared for equality against the entries of the array.

--- src/paste.c
+++ src/paste.c
@@ -52,13 +52,13 @@
 /* Indicates that no delimiter should be added in the current position. */
 #define EMPTY_DELIM '\0'
 
-static FILE dummy_closed;
+static char dummy_closed;
 /* Element marking a file that has reached EOF and been closed. */
-#define CLOSED (&dummy_closed)
+#define CLOSED ((FILE *) &dummy_closed)
 
-static FILE dummy_endlist;
+static char dummy_endlist;
 /* Element marking end of list of open files. */
-#define ENDLIST (&dummy_endlist)
+#define ENDLIST ((FILE *) &dummy_endlist)
 
 /* Name this program was run with. */
 char *program_name;
